# Development history in git-meta

The local git-meta archive explains how slide-engine was built and links that
account to the development conversations, changes, branches, images, and recorded
checks. It lives in `.git/git-meta.sqlite` and serializes to
`refs/meta/local/main`. Image payloads and their indexes use `local:` keys and
are excluded from serialization. Transcripts contain local image references,
not image bytes. Neither is checked into the source tree. No metadata remote
is automatically configured or published.

Start here:

```sh
git-meta get project history:overview
git-meta get project history:coverage
git-meta get project history:branch-index
git-meta get branch:scalar-visualizer
git-meta get branch:metadata-popup
git-meta get commit:df82c3504 agent:summary
git-meta get commit:df82c3504 history:evidence
git-meta get path:src/compiler.mjs description
git-meta tui
```

## What is stored

| Target/key | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| `project history:overview` | Retrospective narrative, milestones and source references |
| `project history:milestone:<name>` | Goals, decisions, evidence and related commits |
| `project history:coverage` | Counts, unresolved provenance and known omissions |
| `project history:verification-evidence` | Historical tool output, including failures; not current test attestations |
| `project codex:session:<id>:info` | Source-prefix hash, cutoff, counts, known models and normalization accounting |
| `project codex:session:<id>:summary` | Human-readable account of the session |
| `project codex:session:<id>:transcript:index` | Ordered chunk keys, encoding and checksums |
| `project codex:session:<id>:transcript:part:<number>` | Bounded UTF-8 chunks that reconstruct normalized JSONL |
| `project local:codex:asset:<sha256>:index` | Local-only chunk index for one content-addressed image |
| `project local:codex:asset:<sha256>:part:<number>` | Local-only image payload chunks |
| `project local:history:asset-index` | Local-only image inventory |
| `branch:<name>` | Purpose, lifecycle evidence, parents, session links and commit membership |
| `commit:<full SHA>` | Change record, summary, provenance, request candidates and session references |
| `change-id:<id>` | Mapping from stable GitButler change IDs to observed commit SHAs |
| `path:<path>` | Responsibility, relevant commits, sessions, and selected documentation snapshots |

Project session archives use custom `codex:` keys because a session can span many
branches and commits. Each transcript is stored once, then referenced from those
targets. The standard `agent:summary`, `description`, `status`, and `branch-id`
keys are used where their meanings fit. `branch-id` records a confirmed original
branch name; it does not claim to be a recovered GitButler branch UUID.

Chunks are strings written with `git-meta set --file`, not command-line-sized
JSONL arguments. The index orders the chunks; concatenation reconstructs the
JSONL byte-for-byte. Chunk boundaries may fall inside a JSONL record. Image
chunks use base64. Checksums cover encoded chunks, the combined encoded value,
and the original decoded image. Lists/sets of links do not duplicate transcripts.
Image chunks, indexes, and image details all remain under `local:`; git-meta
never serializes that namespace. A recipient can read the transcript's image
references but cannot retrieve the images. Local image exports remain available.

## Evidence and limitations

Every source reference includes session ID, source line, event ID and timestamp.
Recorded commit creation and amendments are stronger evidence than merely seeing
a commit in a status output. `history:origin-commit-ids` on a branch includes
recorded creations; `history:commit-ids` also includes observed stack ancestry.
Request candidates are explicitly labeled text-similarity inferences. They are
navigation aids, not proof of authorship. A finishing session can commit edits
made in earlier sessions.

The import retains ordinary user/assistant messages and tool calls/results.
It excludes internal reasoning, system/developer messages, runtime bookkeeping,
and duplicated compaction/event mirrors. Embedded images are extracted and
deduplicated. Home-directory prefixes become `$HOME`; recognized credential
patterns are redacted and counted. Original logs remain unchanged. The import
is a snapshot through an explicit cutoff, including for the session doing the
import. Missing image placeholders in imported conversations cannot be recovered.

The Scalar conversation imported from an external agent is preserved separately
from the CLI session. Its commit trailer self-reports an external model; that
is recorded as reported provenance rather than assigning an unverified runtime
model. The early checkpoint commits have weak operation attribution. See the
coverage record for the exact gaps.

## Refresh and verify

Requires Python 3.9+, the installed `git-meta` CLI, and GitButler `but`. Run from
the primary repository workspace (a `.git` directory is expected):

```sh
python3 scripts/metadata/history.py prepare
python3 scripts/metadata/history.py build
python3 scripts/metadata/history.py apply
python3 scripts/metadata/history.py verify
git-meta serialize
python3 scripts/metadata/roundtrip.py
```

`prepare` discovers project sessions in the local Codex index and archive folders,
captures complete JSONL records up to each file's initial size, and inventories
GitButler history. `build` produces a reviewable plan with retrospective narrative
and evidence links. `apply` compares each value before writing and only adds
missing set members. Reapplying an unchanged plan performs zero writes. `verify`
reads every planned value back through git-meta. These commands never push or
rewrite source commits. Existing unrelated metadata is preserved.

`roundtrip.py` initializes a temporary repository with GitButler, fetches the
local `refs/meta/local/main` archive using git-meta, materializes it into an
independent database, verifies all shared values, and asserts that both `local:`
keys and the former public image namespace are absent. Its remote points only
to this filesystem; the source repository's remote configuration is unchanged.

The default staging directory is `.git/codex-metadata`. `--directory` can select
another location; `--codex-home` selects a different local Codex archive, and
`--ref` selects the history tip for preparation. The narrative module contains
project-specific descriptions and source references; maintain those as the
project evolves. Link sets are append-only historical observations. Updated
transcript indexes supersede old trailing chunks without deleting prior data.

Export a reconstructed transcript or image:

```sh
python3 scripts/metadata/history.py export \
  --key codex:session:01a0a97e-9e6f-7410-ac4d-4cf241343851:transcript \
  --output /tmp/slide-engine-session.jsonl
```

Use `local:codex:asset:<sha256>` as the export key for an image. Export verifies chunk
and complete-value hashes before writing. Archive behavior and evidence parsing
have focused tests:

```sh
python3 -m unittest discover -s scripts/metadata -p 'test_*.py' -v
```

Publishing the archive is a separate operation. Inspect its actual contents and
size, choose the metadata destination, and only then configure that remote.

The initial import's public image keys were migrated to `local:`. The old keys
were removed and the unpublished local metadata ref was rebuilt without its
image-bearing parent history. Merely renaming keys and serializing again would
leave the old images reachable through that history. Original Codex logs and
local image files were retained. Future imports use private image keys directly;
`build` also upgrades references in snapshots prepared by the original importer.
